WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.102 --> 00:00:02.310
JUSTIN JACKSON: "And the
Lord God called to the human

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.830
and said to him, 'Where
are you?'" What a beautiful

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.690
question, where are you?

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.630
And this is this existential
question for Adam now.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.380
"Now the serpent
was most cunning

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.090
of all the beasts of the field
that the Lord God had made.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:22.810
And he said to the woman,
'Though God said you shall not

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:25.150
eat from any tree of the
garden--' And the woman said

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:28.300
to the serpent, 'From the fruit
of the gardens trees we may

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.340
eat, but from the fruit
of the tree in the midst

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.040
of the garden, God has said 'You
shall not eat from it and you

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.350
shall not touch
it, lest you die.'

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:36.940
And the serpent
said to the woman,

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:38.447
'You shall not be doomed to die.

00:00:38.447 --> 00:00:40.280
For God knows that on
the day you eat of it,

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:41.530
your eyes will be open.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:45.850
And you will become as gods,
knowing good and evil.'

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:48.045
And the woman saw that the
tree was good for eating

00:00:48.045 --> 00:00:49.420
and that it was
lust to the eyes.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:51.250
And the tree was
lovely to look at.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:52.930
And she took of
its fruit and ate.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:55.480
And she also gave to
her man, and he ate.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.220
And the eyes of the
two were opened,

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:58.990
and they knew they
were naked, and they

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:03.430
sewed fig leaves and made
themselves loincloths."

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:06.310
So let's just begin, first and
foremost, with the narrative

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:08.440
that we know all so well.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.380
God gave Adam and
Eve a commandment.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.180
They're prideful
desire to become

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.880
like gods is precisely
what allows them

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.370
to be tempted by the serpent.

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:20.320
And giving in to
this temptation,

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.240
they break a commandment
of God and thus

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.820
break their relationship
with the divine.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.550
And as a consequence of
this, God, of course,

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:29.680
escorts them out of the garden.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.860
And in some ways, we read
this as just response.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.120
They had a commandment.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.620
They broke the commandment.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:37.960
They broke their
relationship with the divine.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:41.590
And now, of course, they
have to live their life quite

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:43.810
differently now in this world.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.300
"And they heard the sound
of the Lord God walking

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.520
about in the garden
in the evening breeze,

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.040
and the human and his
woman hid from the Lord God

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.050
in the midst of the
trees of the garden.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:55.390
And the Lord God called to
the human and said to him,

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:57.580
'Where are you?'" What
a beautiful question.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.230
Where are you?

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:02.320
And this is this existential
question for Adam now.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:03.260
Just think about it.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:06.422
He's hiding from his creator,
the one to whom he was

00:02:06.422 --> 00:02:07.630
called to be in relationship.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:09.365
He's now hiding from him.

00:02:09.365 --> 00:02:11.740
"And he said, 'I heard your
sound in the garden and I was

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.990
afraid, for I was
naked, and I hid.'

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:16.420
And he said, 'Who told
you that you were naked?'

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:20.520
From the tree I commanded you
not to eat have you eaten!'" In

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.490
some ways, we can look back
to the various traditions,

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.340
both Rabbinic and Christian, and
they understand these questions

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.140
in a very precise way.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:33.740
And this is God calling
Adam and Eve to repentance.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:36.290
Look, the narrative
here-- let's just boil it

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:38.400
down to something simple.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:39.410
There are commandments.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.120
In this case,
there's one command.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.900
One is to keep it, to stay in
relationship to the divine.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.220
Follow the commandments.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.170
If you don't follow
the commandments,

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.450
simply turn back to God
and say, I'm sorry God.

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:52.370
I did not follow
the commandments.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:53.870
And then, of course,
you'll be asked

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:56.270
to follow the commandments.

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:57.860
It's a really simple
narrative that we

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:00.170
can see all throughout
these biblical texts.

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:02.930
And in particular,
here, "And he said, 'Who

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:05.270
told you that you were naked?

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:08.510
From the tree that I commanded
you not to eat you have eaten!'

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:09.800
And the human said--

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.230
here's his chance to repent--

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:15.230
'The woman whom you gave by
me, she gave me from the tree,

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:16.470
and I ate.'"

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:19.250
Now, all of a sudden, I think
we can see what's going on here.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:20.990
If we're talking
about the intersection

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:23.390
of the divine and human
relationship, combined

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:26.110
with the human and
human relationship,

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:30.340
then what we see here is a
rupture of both simultaneously.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.880
Adam, who has his life,
who names himself only

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:37.390
after this woman
who's taken from him

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:40.690
his very self, who gives
him a sense of personhood.

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:43.970
This sustainer beside him,
notice what he's willing to do.

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:44.860
He cuts her off.

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:46.300
And his language is perfect.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.530
"The woman whom you gave to me."

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:52.030
In a double sense, he's
passing off this responsibility

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:54.370
to his wife and now to God.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:57.430
So we see a
simultaneous disruption

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:00.520
of the divine relationship and
the human relationship here.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:02.020
"And the Lord God
said to the woman,

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:03.370
'What is this you have done?'

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:05.800
And the woman said, 'The
serpent beguiled me,

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:09.550
and I ate.'" Notice
what she does here,

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:11.290
passes blame off to the serpent.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:15.250
She doesn't even mention that
she gave the fruit to Adam

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:16.329
and he ate.

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.190
She doesn't even mention this.

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:20.140
So let's just go
back a little bit

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:23.280
and ask a couple of questions.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.610
What is the state that
Adam and Eve are now

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:29.420
in with regards to their
relationship to the divine?

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:32.030
They stand in front of
him, naked and ashamed.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:34.220
Whereas before, they were
naked and did not know it.

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:36.230
Because they've had
this double knowledge,

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:38.150
they've transgressed
and are conscious

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:40.220
of their transgression.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.867
Now, their state
is one of shame.

00:04:42.867 --> 00:04:44.450
And now, you can
really start thinking

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.750
about justice and mercy.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:49.150
Would not justice
be God saying, I

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:52.970
told you not to eat of the tree
of knowledge and good and evil?

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:55.010
And yet you've done so.

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:58.880
And so now, you shall just have
the fruit of your transgression

00:04:58.880 --> 00:04:59.780
here.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:01.820
You shall now live
in this shame which

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:03.900
you've brought upon yourself.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:06.560
And so when we look here,
"Now that the human has become

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.740
like one of us,
knowing good and evil,

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:11.840
let us not let them take
from the tree of life

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:12.590
and live forever."

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:17.730
God is not afraid of a
rivalry of his creation.

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:19.670
He's not afraid that
they've become gods.

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:23.180
He set everything in place so
that they could become godlike.

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:27.770
I mean, really, as a God who
created mud, he's created soil,

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:29.360
and he's turned them into gods.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:32.870
Is a God who has that sort of
enormous power really going

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:37.680
to rival these godlike creatures
that he's created from mud?

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:41.130
Rather, it was a graciousness on
the part of the divine to say,

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:44.820
I am going to create creatures
who, with their will,

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:48.560
can live and participate
in the divine life.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:51.710
And whereas at one point
they had one commandment

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:55.970
to do so, well, now, OK, we
have the original transgression.

00:05:55.970 --> 00:06:00.020
Now, they have 1,613
commandments to do so.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:03.380
And those 1,613 commandments
should be seen as a joy,

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:08.330
as another opportunity to
exist in that divine and human

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:10.860
relationship.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.580
So then what we see here
then, cutting them off

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:16.610
from the Garden of Eden,
to put the cherubim

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:19.250
at that tree of life, to cut
them off from immortality,

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:22.250
to become these godlike
creatures living

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:25.400
in shame in perpetuity,
rather than an act of justice,

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:26.810
it is an act of mercy.

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:29.600
Rather than letting them,
by their own free will,

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:34.170
create a hell out of a paradise
which he's given to them,

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:36.050
he has cut them off
from that and is

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:39.950
going to now allow them to exist
in relationship to the divine

00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:41.900
in a different manner.

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.060
And the rest of
Genesis is going to be

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:46.760
a teasing out, and
through the whole Tanakh--

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:48.110
through the whole Hebrew Bible--

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:50.360
is going to be a
question of what

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:53.880
does that new
relationship look like?

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.330
SPEAKER: Thank you for watching.

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:57.300
We hope you're enjoying
our highlight series

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:00.210
and invite you to explore
all of Hillsdale College's

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:01.380
online courses.

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:05.540
They are free and for
everyone who loves to learn.

